WEBVTT

00:00:10.718 --> 00:00:12.136
( bell dings )

00:00:17.517 --> 00:00:19.185
( door creaks )

00:00:20.603 --> 00:00:21.562
( smacking )

00:00:21.604 --> 00:00:22.605
Augustus,

00:00:22.689 --> 00:00:24.440
please don’t eat your fingers.

00:00:24.482 --> 00:00:26.943
But I taste so good.

00:00:36.744 --> 00:00:38.413
♪ ♪

00:00:39.831 --> 00:00:42.542
Look, Mother,
I’m much more flexible now.

00:00:42.625 --> 00:00:45.837
Yes, but you’re blue.

00:00:47.755 --> 00:00:50.758
( flies buzzing )

00:00:59.851 --> 00:01:01.477
♪ ♪

00:01:07.984 --> 00:01:10.903
Daddy, I want
a flying glass elevator.

00:01:10.987 --> 00:01:13.322
Veruca, the only thing
you’re getting today is a bath

00:01:13.364 --> 00:01:14.657
and that’s final.

00:01:14.699 --> 00:01:16.159
But I want it!

00:01:27.462 --> 00:01:29.756
♪ ♪

00:01:32.633 --> 00:01:35.762
Where do you live?

00:01:35.845 --> 00:01:38.848
Right over there,
that little house.

00:01:40.892 --> 00:01:43.019
( humming )

00:01:43.102 --> 00:01:44.854
What time do you think
they’ll be back?

00:01:44.896 --> 00:01:46.689
I don’t know, dear.

00:01:46.773 --> 00:01:48.024
Hm.

00:01:48.065 --> 00:01:49.901
( crashing,
Mrs. Bucket screams )

00:01:55.990 --> 00:01:58.701
( debris clattering )

00:01:58.785 --> 00:02:01.746
I think there’s someone
at the door.

00:02:01.829 --> 00:02:02.872
Hi, Mom!

00:02:02.914 --> 00:02:04.415
( bell dings )

00:02:04.499 --> 00:02:05.625
Hi.

00:02:05.708 --> 00:02:06.709
CHARLIE:
Mom!

00:02:06.793 --> 00:02:07.710
Dad! We’re back.

00:02:07.752 --> 00:02:08.711
MR. BUCKET:
Charlie.

00:02:08.753 --> 00:02:10.004
MRS. BUCKET:
Charlie.

00:02:10.922 --> 00:02:13.132
Goodness.

00:02:14.842 --> 00:02:16.093
This is Willy Wonka.

00:02:16.177 --> 00:02:17.887
He gave us a ride home.

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:19.180
I see that.

00:02:19.222 --> 00:02:22.683
You must be the boy’s...

00:02:22.725 --> 00:02:24.519
Parents?
Yeah.

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:26.229
That.
He says

00:02:26.312 --> 00:02:29.023
Charlie’s
won something.

00:02:29.857 --> 00:02:31.692
Not just some something,

00:02:31.734 --> 00:02:32.860
the most something
something

00:02:32.902 --> 00:02:34.237
of any something
that’s ever

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:36.030
been.

00:02:36.072 --> 00:02:39.367
I’m gonna give this little boy
my entire factory.

00:02:40.743 --> 00:02:42.453
You must
be joking.

00:02:42.537 --> 00:02:43.830
WONKA:
No, really,

00:02:43.913 --> 00:02:45.498
it’s true,
’cause, you see,

00:02:45.581 --> 00:02:46.666
a few months ago,

00:02:46.749 --> 00:02:48.251
I was having
my semi-annual haircut,

00:02:48.292 --> 00:02:51.379
and I had the strangest
revelation.

00:02:59.887 --> 00:03:02.139
♪ ♪

00:03:05.059 --> 00:03:07.270
WONKA ( voice-over ):
In that one silver hair,

00:03:07.353 --> 00:03:09.355
I saw reflected my life’s work,

00:03:09.438 --> 00:03:12.400
my factory,
my beloved Oompa-Loompas.

00:03:12.441 --> 00:03:16.279
Who would watch over them
after I was gone?

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:18.823
I realized in that moment...

00:03:18.906 --> 00:03:21.784
I must find a heir.

00:03:21.826 --> 00:03:25.246
And I did, Charlie.

00:03:25.288 --> 00:03:27.623
You.

00:03:27.707 --> 00:03:28.791
( sighs happily )

00:03:28.833 --> 00:03:30.126
CHARLIE:
That’s why you sent out

00:03:30.167 --> 00:03:31.419
the golden tickets.

00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:32.962
Uh-huh.

00:03:33.045 --> 00:03:34.463
( whispers ):
What are Oompa-Loompas?

00:03:34.505 --> 00:03:35.506
WONKA:
I invited

00:03:35.590 --> 00:03:36.883
five children to the factory,

00:03:36.966 --> 00:03:38.217
and the one
who was the least

00:03:38.301 --> 00:03:40.136
rotten would be the winner.

00:03:40.219 --> 00:03:41.846
GRANDPA JOE:
That’s you, Charlie.

00:03:41.929 --> 00:03:43.139
WONKA:
So, what do you say?

00:03:43.222 --> 00:03:45.182
Are you ready
to leave all this behind

00:03:45.266 --> 00:03:47.310
and come live with me
at the factory?

00:03:47.351 --> 00:03:49.020
Sure. Of course.

00:03:49.103 --> 00:03:50.646
I mean, it’s all right
for my family

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:51.981
to come, too.

00:03:52.023 --> 00:03:54.191
Oh, my dear boy,
of course they can’t.

00:03:54.275 --> 00:03:56.235
You can’t run
a chocolate factory

00:03:56.319 --> 00:03:59.447
with a family hanging over you
like an old dead goose.

00:03:59.488 --> 00:04:00.615
No offense.

00:04:00.656 --> 00:04:02.617
None taken... jerk.

00:04:04.243 --> 00:04:06.495
A chocolatier
has to run free and solo.

00:04:06.537 --> 00:04:08.831
He has to follow his dreams,
gosh darn

00:04:08.915 --> 00:04:10.333
the consequences.

00:04:11.542 --> 00:04:12.543
Look at me.

00:04:12.627 --> 00:04:15.504
I had no family,
and I’m a giant success.

00:04:15.546 --> 00:04:18.758
So, if I go with you
to the factory,

00:04:18.841 --> 00:04:21.218
I won’t ever see
my family again?

00:04:21.302 --> 00:04:22.136
Yeah.

00:04:22.178 --> 00:04:23.596
Consider that a bonus.

00:04:26.015 --> 00:04:27.266
Then I’m not going.

00:04:27.350 --> 00:04:29.435
I wouldn’t give
up my family

00:04:29.518 --> 00:04:30.519
for anything.

00:04:30.561 --> 00:04:33.564
Not for all the chocolate
in the world.

00:04:35.691 --> 00:04:37.944
Oh, I see.

00:04:39.195 --> 00:04:40.696
That’s weird.

00:04:40.738 --> 00:04:42.990
( weak laugh )

00:04:44.450 --> 00:04:47.662
There’s other candy, too,
besides chocolate.

00:04:47.703 --> 00:04:49.956
I’m sorry, Mr. Wonka.

00:04:50.039 --> 00:04:51.666
I’m staying here.

00:04:53.918 --> 00:04:56.170
Wow.

00:04:57.880 --> 00:05:00.716
Well, that’s just...

00:05:00.758 --> 00:05:03.594
unexpected and...

00:05:03.678 --> 00:05:05.721
weird.

00:05:12.478 --> 00:05:14.981
But I suppose, in that case,
I’ll just...

00:05:17.650 --> 00:05:19.151
Good-bye then.

00:05:22.071 --> 00:05:24.281
Sure you won’t change your mind?

00:05:25.908 --> 00:05:28.452
I’m sure.

00:05:37.128 --> 00:05:39.714
Okay. Bye.

00:05:39.755 --> 00:05:41.632
( bell dings )

00:05:45.803 --> 00:05:48.597
( rumbling )

00:05:48.639 --> 00:05:50.725
( whooshing )

00:06:00.359 --> 00:06:04.238
Things are going
to get much better.

00:06:04.280 --> 00:06:06.824
NARRATOR:
And, for once,

00:06:06.907 --> 00:06:08.451
Grandma Georgina knew exactly

00:06:08.492 --> 00:06:10.745
what she was talking about.

00:06:10.786 --> 00:06:12.246
The next morning,

00:06:12.288 --> 00:06:15.708
Charlie helped his parents
fix a hole in the roof.

00:06:15.791 --> 00:06:19.962
Grandpa Joe spent the whole day
out of bed.

00:06:20.004 --> 00:06:22.256
He didn’t feel tired at all.

00:06:22.298 --> 00:06:25.342
Charlie’s father got
a better job

00:06:25.426 --> 00:06:27.303
at the toothpaste factory,

00:06:27.386 --> 00:06:30.473
repairing the machine
that had replaced him.

00:06:32.683 --> 00:06:35.811
Things have never been better
for the Bucket family.

00:06:35.895 --> 00:06:40.483
The same could not be said
for Willy Wonka.

00:06:40.524 --> 00:06:42.443
I can’t put my finger on it.

00:06:42.485 --> 00:06:44.195
Candy’s always been
the only thing

00:06:44.278 --> 00:06:45.529
I was ever certain of,

00:06:45.613 --> 00:06:47.448
and now I’m just
not certain at all.

00:06:47.490 --> 00:06:49.867
I don’t know which
flavors to make,

00:06:49.950 --> 00:06:51.160
I don’t know which ideas to try.

00:06:51.243 --> 00:06:52.578
I’m second-guessing myself,

00:06:52.661 --> 00:06:53.871
which is nuts.

00:06:53.954 --> 00:06:58.793
I’ve always made whatever
candy I felt like, and I...

00:07:04.924 --> 00:07:06.175
That’s just it, isn’t it?

00:07:06.217 --> 00:07:08.511
I make the candy I feel like,
but now

00:07:08.552 --> 00:07:10.012
I feel terrible,
so the candy’s

00:07:10.096 --> 00:07:12.306
terrible.

00:07:12.348 --> 00:07:14.183
You’re very good.

